WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.479
[INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC]

00:00:33.892 --> 00:00:34.850
ZISL SLEPOVITCH: Hello.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:38.270
My name is Zisl Slepovitch,
and I'm a musician-in-residence

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:41.780
at Yale University's Fortunoff
Video Archive for Holocaust

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:43.130
Testimonies.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:45.800
This video series is
designed as a companion

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.170
to our album, "Where
is our Homeland?"

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.320
which is the first volume in
the Archive's ongoing effort

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:54.080
to present songs
and poems performed

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.770
by survivors in testimonies
given to the Fortunoff Archive

00:00:57.770 --> 00:00:59.990
over the last 40 years.

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:01.640
You can learn more
about the project

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.970
on the Archive's website,
songsfromtestimonies.org.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:07.940
"Where is our Homeland?

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:10.130
Songs from
Testimonies, Volume 1"

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:14.570
was recorded in 2019 by the
Zisl Slepovitch Ensemble,

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:17.730
featuring renowned
singer Sasha Lurje,

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:21.440
Craig Judelman on the violin,
Joshua Camp on the accordion,

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:25.250
keyboards, and guitar,
Dimitry Ishenko on the bass,

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:27.800
and myself on woodwinds.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.900
The songs in this
collection differ vastly

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:33.380
in terms of style, social
and historical background,

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.730
language, and many
other aspects.

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:39.320
But collectively, they create
a multidimensional picture

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:44.680
of Jewish life before
and during World War II.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:49.420
Today we'll talk about the song
that was written in German,

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:52.710
although the name is in
Hebrew, "Kadima," "Forward,"

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.340
Vorwarts in German.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:59.260
And it was sung by
Itzchak S. Itzchak

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:03.580
was born in 1915
in Berlin, Germany.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:07.660
He actively participated in
Jewish Zionist youth groups

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:13.240
and, according to his testimony,
established one himself.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.820
In the interview,
given in Hebrew,

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:19.450
Itzchak recalls the
song, allegedly composed

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:24.880
by Akiva Lewinsky, born
in 1918 and died in 2000,

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.520
a prominent Zionist leader.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.920
Here's a short clip
from his testimony.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:36.810
ITZCHAK S.: but we
also adopted an anthem,

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:41.060
and it was a song
that was written

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:50.740
by Akiva Levinsky, that lives
today in the Ma'ayan Zvi group.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:56.220
He was one of the leaders of our
youth group in the good days,

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.780
he made Aliyah fairly
early, it is though

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:03.740
a song in the German language.

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:08.820
The song says forward,
we will march forward,

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:15.740
we don't have time
to waste, we know

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:20.020
that the future is with
us, the good future, that

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.260
is roughly the first
stanza, the last stanza is

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.460
that we are fighting
for the honor of Israel,

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:35.580
we want a new generation,
to create, that is the song,

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:40.220
and the tune is of
course unforgettable,

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:43.340
and what is interesting
is that Ivo, when she was

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:46.860
under arrest, in a
prison, told me afterwards

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:51.060
that she hummed the
words of this song,

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:53.900
and that it gave her strength.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:04:01.300
(SINGING) Kadima--Forward, march,
We have no time to lose.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.860
We know, we carry
the future with us,

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.940
So we know we must march on.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:10.020
That is the first
stanza, and the last one

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.340
- We fight for freedom,
equality, and justice, We

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:16.820
fight for the honor of Israel.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.820
We want a new, strong breed.

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:23.540
We demand Jewish honor.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.060
ZISL SLEPOVITCH:
"Kadima," "Vorwarts"

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:31.450
"Forward" is written in
the genre of a solemn,

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:35.380
probably military march,
typical for the anthems

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:38.440
and other officially
sponsored songs in Europe

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:41.320
and the Soviet
Union in the '30s.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:45.170
Very few of the
ideologically charged songs,

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:47.840
be that Zionist,
Bundist, or communist,

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:50.690
were musically
idiomatically Jewish.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:54.130
The composers, most
of them unknown to us,

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:55.900
and the survivors
themselves were

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:59.290
part of the multi-ethnic,
multilingual, and ultimately

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:02.580
cosmopolitan milieu
in which they grew up.

00:05:04.655 --> 00:05:08.134
[INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC]

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:54.300
Kadima--Forward Forward, march,
We have no time to lose.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:58.700
We know, we carry
the future with us,

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:02.940
So we know we must march on.

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:07.140
We know, we carry
the future with us,

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:11.500
So we know we must march on.

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:19.660
[INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC]

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:27.820
We fight for freedom,
equality, and justice,

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:32.140
We fight for the honor of Israel.

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:36.380
We want a new, strong breed.

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:40.620
We demand Jewish honor.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:44.900
We want a new, strong breed.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:49.260
We demand Jewish honor.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:07:06.140
[INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC]

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:21.660
DEREK PENSLAR: When Itzchak
refers to this song,

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:25.700
he describes it as a hymn,
or a hymnon in Hebrew,

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:29.010
that was sung by members of
his German Jewish youth group

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:30.930
when they were in
hiding, or when

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:32.670
they were living
as an underground

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:34.590
during the Second World War.

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:37.050
But he does acknowledge
that it was written earlier,

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:38.620
during the Weimar period.

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:40.800
So let's talk a little
bit about this song

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:44.460
and what it tells us about
German Zionism during the 1920s

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.080
and the early '30s.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.120
Well, first of
all, who wrote it?

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:50.220
Itzchak, in his
testimony, attributes it

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:53.760
to Akiva Lewinsky, who
was an important figure

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:56.290
in the state of Israel during
its first four decades.

00:07:56.290 --> 00:07:59.550
He was a functionary
in the Jewish Agency,

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:02.250
he was the director
of Bank Hapoalim

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:04.660
Then he did a lot of
other important things.

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:06.360
So was he the guy?

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:10.650
Well, we do know that,
according to his biography,

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:14.730
he joined a German Jewish youth
group when he was a child--

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:17.850
when he was 9, evidently--
and that it was called Kadima.

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:20.280
Well, "Kadima" is
the name of the song.

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:24.030
We also know that he wrote
music and poetry as a youth.

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:27.970
Evidently, he wrote poems
from early childhood.

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:30.690
He was also a great aficionado
of Hebrew from an early age,

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:34.369
and was committed to moving
to the Land of Israel.

00:08:34.369 --> 00:08:36.720
But there we have a
little bit of a problem,

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:40.109
in that he moves to the
Land of Israel in 1934,

00:08:40.109 --> 00:08:42.100
when he was 14 years old.

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:44.970
So if he wrote this song,
and he might very well have,

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:49.440
he did it when he was
10, 11, 12, 13 years old.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:51.930
Now, it is a simple
song, so it is possible

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:54.210
that he wrote it then.

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:56.645
And in the testimony, Itzchak
sings it in both Hebrew

00:08:56.645 --> 00:08:58.020
and in German,
and I wonder if it

00:08:58.020 --> 00:08:59.920
was written in both languages.

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:03.300
So we can't be 100 percent
sure that Lewinsky wrote it,

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:06.230
but I think there's a
good chance that he did.

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:08.528
He was just a very gifted child.

00:09:08.528 --> 00:09:10.070
Let me say a few
words about the song

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:11.980
itself, and its broader context.

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:13.460
It's a very German song--

00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:17.420
in its cadence, in its
music, and in its language.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:19.730
Some of it's a bit
disturbing, like the reference

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:22.070
to ein neues starkes
Geschlecht you know,

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:23.900
a new and strong
breed of humanity.

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:26.120
But to be fair, that
kind of language

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:30.375
was used not only in the
German world in the 1920s.

00:09:30.375 --> 00:09:32.000
But what's interesting
also is the song

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:33.860
doesn't really say
anything specifically

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:39.470
about Jews, or about Judaism,
or about Aliyah, or about Eretz

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:40.220
Israel.

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:43.310
But there is something it refers
to twice-- it refers to honor,

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:46.640
or Ehre, Juedische Ehre
or the Ehre of Israel--

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:48.560
that is, the Jewish people.

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:50.483
And that's not a coincidence.

00:09:50.483 --> 00:09:52.400
The Zionist movement,
from the very beginning,

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.920
was very concerned with honor.

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:56.480
And one doesn't
have to look only

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:58.680
at German Zionism for this.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:00.920
Lev Pinsker, who
was from Odessa,

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:03.860
published a famous
pamphlet in 1882 called

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:05.300
"Self-Emancipation."

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:07.090
And it was very
much about honor--

00:10:07.090 --> 00:10:09.650
about how the Jews need
to honor themselves

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:13.370
so they can be honored
by the rest of the world.

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:16.340
Honor and respect were central
in the Zionist writings

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:18.130
of Theodor Herzl.

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:22.660
So this is a theme that goes
back long before this song was

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:23.320
written.

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:30.610
And indeed, there were German
Zionist hymns of the 1910s,

00:10:30.610 --> 00:10:33.220
the turn of the century,
and even earlier.

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:37.260
Theodor Herzl's favorite Zionist
hymn had been written in 1885,

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:39.490
but it was quite
popular in his own time.

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:41.440
And it has language
that in some ways

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:43.750
is quite different from
the song we're looking at,

00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:46.300
but there's a
certain commonality.

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.280
I want to read just a
few lines from this poem.

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:50.620
It was called "There
Where the Cedar,"

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:52.680
and it begins like this.

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:55.900
There where the slender
cedar kisses the clouds,

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:58.870
and where the
Jordan's rapids flow,

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:01.570
there where my
father's ashes rest,

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:05.517
the Earth has drunk
the blood of Maccabees.

00:11:05.517 --> 00:11:07.600
Now, the reference to
Maccabees is important here.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:10.570
Akiva Lewinsky, we know that
he belonged to a youth group

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:13.690
called Kadima, which is
the name of this song.

00:11:13.690 --> 00:11:16.360
Well, that youth group merged
with another group called

00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:20.050
Tsofim or Scouts, and
it formed a new group

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:22.270
called Young Maccabees.

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:25.660
Comparing Jews to Maccabees,
to ancient warriors,

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:27.970
was found throughout
modern Jewish history,

00:11:27.970 --> 00:11:32.260
but it's particularly
powerful in German Zionism.

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:36.010
And it goes back to 1914, when
the economist and sociologist

00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:41.140
Franz Oppenheimer wrote an essay
called Alte und neue Makkabeer

00:11:41.140 --> 00:11:44.180
"Old and New Maccabees."

00:11:44.180 --> 00:11:46.760
Now, let me now
talk a little bit

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:51.800
about the background of
German Zionism of the 1920s.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:53.510
On the first tape
of the testimony--

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:55.790
there's five different
tapes-- but on the first one,

00:11:55.790 --> 00:11:58.430
Itzchak refers to
himself joining

00:11:58.430 --> 00:12:01.650
Kadima when he was a child.

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:03.110
Now, this was an
organization that

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:06.260
grew out of a more famous
German Jewish youth group

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:10.670
called Blau-Weiss or Blue-White
that had been founded in 1912.

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:12.920
And it collapsed in 1926--

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:15.800
internal politics,
and youth groups

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:18.770
often sort of came and
went in Weimar Germany.

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:20.780
But what happened
over time, first

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:25.130
in Blau-Weiss, and then Kadima,
is that the movement's focus

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:26.270
changed.

00:12:26.270 --> 00:12:28.700
Like the German youth
movement as a whole,

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:30.560
in the early 20th
century, the emphasis

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:32.990
was on individual cultivation--

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:35.510
personal experience
with nature, long

00:12:35.510 --> 00:12:38.810
rambles through the forests,
music, and so forth.

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:40.740
In Germany and among
German Jewish youth

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:44.600
as well, as we get further into
the 1920s and the early '30s,

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:47.252
the youth movements
become more nationalistic.

00:12:47.252 --> 00:12:48.710
And for the Jewish
youth movements,

00:12:48.710 --> 00:12:52.170
a greater emphasis not just
on Zionism, but on Aliyah,

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:56.330
on immigration to Eretz Israel,
and Hakhshara preparation

00:12:56.330 --> 00:12:59.990
for life in the Land of Israel
as a laborer, particularly

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:01.220
on a kibbutz.

00:13:01.220 --> 00:13:03.590
Kadima was part of
a whole umbrella

00:13:03.590 --> 00:13:05.720
group of German
Jewish youth groups

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:08.250
that had about 2,000
young people in all.

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:10.100
And I just want to
read you a sentence

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:13.490
from the founding statement
of this umbrella organization,

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:16.100
because it really gives you
a sense of what German youth

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:17.750
groups, German
Jewish youth groups,

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:20.990
were all about when you
get into the later '20s.

00:13:20.990 --> 00:13:23.660
"We firmly believe
that in Palestine,

00:13:23.660 --> 00:13:26.990
the cornerstone for a new
society is being laid.

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:31.520
We cannot do a thing here except
to prepare ourselves to become

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:34.730
worthy helpers of the
Palestinian workers,

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:38.030
and to teach the German Jewish
youth the ideal of a new

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:40.660
and dignified life
in Eretz Israel."

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:42.650
Note the emphasis not
only on labor, but also

00:13:42.650 --> 00:13:44.980
on dignity, which
gets us back to honor

00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:47.050
that I talked about earlier.

00:13:47.050 --> 00:13:48.800
Now, how we judge the
seriousness of these

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:52.320
statements really
depends on perspective.

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:56.300
There were about
2,000 German Jewish

00:13:56.300 --> 00:13:58.670
youth in these organizations.

00:13:58.670 --> 00:14:01.280
Hundreds of them did wind
up moving to Eretz Israel

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:02.600
in the 1920s.

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:07.370
About 2,000 German Jews in
all moved to Eretz Israel.

00:14:07.370 --> 00:14:10.850
That might not sound like many,
but there were 20,000 German

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:15.320
Jews in Zionistiche Vereinigung
für Deutschland the German

00:14:15.320 --> 00:14:16.660
Zionist Organization.

00:14:16.660 --> 00:14:18.590
And that's actually
quite a lot when

00:14:18.590 --> 00:14:21.410
we think about the Jewish
population of Germany

00:14:21.410 --> 00:14:23.210
being about half a million.

00:14:23.210 --> 00:14:26.120
In the United States, which
had eight times as many Jews--

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:28.490
more than four million Jews--

00:14:28.490 --> 00:14:32.810
there were about 60,000 Jews
total in the American Zionist

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:37.100
organizations, so three
times as many as in Germany,

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:39.620
but the community was
eight times larger.

00:14:39.620 --> 00:14:44.510
And also, the American community
was about 13,000 members

00:14:44.510 --> 00:14:46.130
of the American
Zionist Organization,

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:48.230
and the rest were in
Hadassah the women's Zionist

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:48.950
organization.

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:50.540
So it was split.

00:14:50.540 --> 00:14:53.120
Immigration to the
land of Israel was low.

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:55.310
So actually, German Zionism
was much more committed

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:59.030
to Eretz Israel than
American Zionism.

00:14:59.030 --> 00:15:02.150
And German Zionism was
more popular in Germany

00:15:02.150 --> 00:15:04.610
than American Zionism
was in the United States.

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:06.530
And that might be
counter-intuitive.

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:10.192
But I think it's important
to keep that in mind.

00:15:10.192 --> 00:15:12.400
The German Jewish world that
gave birth to this song,

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:16.060
then, was one where Zionism
was pretty prevalent--

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:17.920
not dominant, prevalent.

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:20.500
And within Zionism, there
was a serious commitment

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:27.610
to immigration, even if not all
the time realized in principle.

00:15:27.610 --> 00:15:29.440
The fact that the
song itself doesn't

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:32.440
refer to the Land of Israel
is a bit of a red herring.

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:35.230
It may have been written by,
again, a very young man, still

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:39.970
a boy, whose own thinking was
in the process of formation.

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:42.310
Or perhaps, if it was
written by someone else,

00:15:42.310 --> 00:15:44.800
it was written at a time
when the youth movement was

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:47.380
undergoing transformations
and debates

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:50.260
about how important the
Eretz Israel would be to it.

00:15:50.260 --> 00:15:52.558
All this to say that we
can learn an awful lot

00:15:52.558 --> 00:15:53.350
from a little song.

00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:57.900
[INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC]

